  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   We address the Euler's minimum overlap problem by optimizing a step function h(x) defined on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum correlation between h and 1-h. The key insight is that a higher-resolution representation can capture more complex structures, leading to a lower C5 value. Our approach uses generation optimization with a population of points evolved over generations, where each generation's mean correlation is computed.\n\n   We start by initializing a population with samples from the provided parent solution (45 points) and define the fitness as the maximum correlation (C5). Each generation consists of local optimal search around the current best solution, followed by global crossover and mutation. This hybrid approach aims to explore both exploitation and exploration effectively.\n\n   The algorithm includes a restart mechanism using a different initial solution if performance stagnates. The complexity is manageable due to the optimization budget and population timing management.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   We represent h(x) as a discrete vector in [0,1] with integral 1. For a N-point representation, the integral constraints require sum(h) = N/2.\n\n   The objective is to minimize:\n        C5 = (2/N) * max_{k} correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k]\n\n   The hypersteps are:\n        Population initialization: 10 numbers from the parent or random uniform [0,1]\n        Fitness evaluation: Compute C5 using correlation and mean of the top 10%\n        Selection: Pick 5 \u043b\u0443\u0447\u0448\u0438\u0445 individuals (highest fitness) for the next generation\n        Local refinement: Run SLSQP optimization with integral and box constraints\n        Crossover and mutation: Apply percentage-based crossover and random mutations\n        Population update: 5\ud0d0 refined + 5 from crossover/mutation\n        Restart: If mean fitness doesn't improve for 5 generations, restart population with new solution\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Helper functions:\n        parent2n(n): Transfer solution from parent to child with N points using FFT smoothing and SLSQP optimization\n        validate_and_scale(h): Ensure h satisfies constraints and normalize integral if necessary\n        evaluate_fitness(h): Compute C5 for given h\n        optimize_local(h): Local optimization using SLSQP\n\n   The main 'run' function:\n        global_initial_h = initial_h_values  # Provided via import\n        dx = 2.0 / n_points\n\n        if global_initial_h is None:\n            generate random initial\n        else:\n            use global_initial_h as base\n\n        Main optimization loop:\n            For each generation (up to 20):\n                - Check for budget expiration\n                - Select best individuals\n                - Perform local optimization\n                - Calculate fitness and record best solution\n                - If stagnation, restart population\n                - Swap population and generate child if better\n                - Keep the best between generations\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Population size: 10\n   - Local optimization iterations: 10\n   - Crossover and mutation rates: Adaptive based on fitness\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n   - Time budget: 1100 seconds divided adaptively per generation\n   - Automated stopping when budget exhausted or early convergence\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Ensure h is in [0,1] and has integral 1\n   - Compute C5 and ensure consistency with evaluation\n   - Return best solution and its fitness\n\nHelpers for efficacy:\nrequire scipy, numpy, cvxpy (if needed)",
